First Author: Chang
Title: Patterns of Left Ventricular Regional Wall Motion Abnormalities After Brain Death and Their Clinical Significance
Source number: 7  ￼

Very short summary: This prospective multicenter Donor Heart Study used unsupervised machine learning to classify regional wall motion abnormality (RWMA) patterns in potential heart donors after brain death. Five key findings were: (1) among 913 analyzed initial echocardiograms with RWMA, 4 phenotypes were identified: focal basal septal (FBS), basal and midventricular, apical, and global hypokinesis; (2) FBS was the most common phenotype (55%); (3) the phenotypes differed in troponin, NT-proBNP, and initial LVEF, with FBS showing the lowest biomarkers and highest LVEF; (4) all phenotypes improved on follow-up echocardiography, with global hypokinesis showing the largest LVEF improvement; and (5) FBS had the highest donor-heart acceptance rate for transplantation, but recipient survival after transplant did not differ significantly by donor RWMA phenotype. Figures 1-3 and Tables 1-2 were directly used for this extraction, along with the article’s funding/disclosure section.

